  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nA large area of high pressure will keep the weather dry for the\nrest of the upcoming week. Mostly clear skies and cool\ntemperatures may lead to patchy frost across north-central\nWisconsin in protected and low laying areas tonight.\n\nNWP models do ram a low pressure system into the high for the\nupcoming weekend. However, as often is the case in these\nscenarios, the high ends up winning and we end up drier or the low\ntracks through a bit later than forecast this far out. Therefore,\nwhile there are some small (20-30%) POPs in the forecast this\nweekend, the expectation is any rain wouldn't be a soaker with a\nchance this system could dry up or be delayed by the Hudson Bay\nhigh that is expected to be in place.\n\nLooking ahead the next chance for any appreciable rain does not\nlook like it would be a possibility until the early to middle part\nof next week when the high retreats, allowing a trough to track\nthrough the western Great Lakes. Again, given the tendency of \nthese high pressure systems, this could be delayed even further.\n\nTemperatures this week will gradually trend warmer each day with \nhighs climbing back into the upper 60s to low 70s by the weekend.",
